"Internal Error ()\nciteturn2306view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2303view1\",\"id\":7}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHunyuanOCR Technical Report (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.19575v1)\nciteturn2306view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2297view3\",\"pattern\":\"C.1\"}); Total lines: 825\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Traditional OCR Systems L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Vision-Language Models L20:       1. cite10†2.2.1 General Vision-Language Models L21:       2. cite11†2.2.2 OCR-Specific Vision-Language Models L22:   4. cite12†3 Model Design L23:   5. cite13†4 Data Construction L24:     1. cite14†4.1 Task Design L25:       1. cite15†4.1.1 Spotting L26:       2. cite16†4.1.2 Parsing L27:       3. cite17†4.1.3 IE & VQA L28:       4. cite18†4.1.4 Text Image Translation L29:     2. cite19†4.2 Data Pipelines L30:       1. cite20†4.2.1 Image data synthesis L31:       2. cite21†4.2.2 Image data augmentation L32:       3. cite22†4.2.3 Question–Answer Pair Generation L33:   6. cite23†5 Training Recipe L34:     1. cite24†5.1 Pre-Training L35:     2. cite25†5.2 Reinforcement Learning (RL) L36:       1. cite26†5.2.1 Data Curation L37:       2. cite27†5.2.2 Reward Design L38:       3. cite28†5.2.3 Training Strategy L39:   7. cite29†6 Evaluation L40:     1. cite30†6.1 Spotting L41:     2. cite31†6.2 Parsing L42:     3. cite32†6.3 IE & VQA L43:     4. cite33†6.4 Text Image Translation L44:   8. cite34†7 Conclusion L45:   9. cite35†References L46:   10. cite36†A Recommended Instruction L47:   11. cite37†B Common Supported IE Categories L48:   12. cite38†C Reinforcement Learning Details L49:     1. cite39†C.1 Training Configuration L50:     2. cite40†C.2 Training Dynamics L51:     3. cite41†C.3 Task-wise Performance Improvements L52:   13. cite42†D Qualitative examples L53: cite43†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L54: \nL55: arXiv:2511.19575v1 [cs.CV] 24 Nov 2025\nL56: # HunyuanOCR Technical Report\nL57: \nL58: Tencent Hunyuan Vision Team\nL59: ###### Abstract\nL60: This paper presents HunyuanOCR, a commercial-grade, open-source, and lightweight (1B parameters) Vision-Language Model (VLM) dedicated to OCR tasks. The architecture comprises a Native Vision Transformer (ViT) and a lightweight LLM connected via an MLP adapter. HunyuanOCR demonstrates superior performance, outperforming commercial APIs, traditional pipelines, and larger models (e.g., Qwen3-VL-4B).\nL61: Specifically, it surpasses current public solutions in perception tasks (Text Spotting, Parsing) and excels in semantic tasks (IE, Text Image Translation), securing first place in the ICDAR 2025 DIMT Challenge (Small Model Track). Furthermore, it achieves state-of-the-art (SOTA) results on OCRBench among VLMs with fewer than 3B parameters.\nL62: HunyuanOCR achieves breakthroughs in three key aspects: 1) Unifying Versatility and Efficiency: We implement comprehensive support for core capabilities including spotting, parsing, IE, VQA, and translation within a lightweight framework. This addresses the limitations of narrow “OCR expert models” and inefficient “General VLMs”. 2) Streamlined End-to-End Architecture: Adopting a pure end-to-end paradigm eliminates dependencies on pre-processing modules (e.g., layout analysis).\nL63: This fundamentally resolves error propagation common in traditional pipelines and simplifies system deployment. 3) Data-Driven and RL Strategies: We confirm the critical role of high-quality data and, for the first time in the industry, demonstrate that Reinforcement Learning (RL) strategies yield significant performance gains in OCR tasks.\nL64: HunyuanOCR is officially open-sourced on HuggingFace. We also provide a high-performance deployment solution based on vLLM, placing its production efficiency in the top tier. We hope this model will advance frontier research and provide a solid foundation for industrial applications.\nL65: \nL66: > cite44†Image: [Uncaptioned image] | cite45†https://huggingface.co/tencent/HunyuanOCR†huggingface.co L67: cite46†Image: [Uncaptioned image] | cite47†https://github.com/Tencent-Hunyuan/HunyuanOCR†github.com L68: cite48†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Performance comparison of HunyuanOCR and other SOTA models.\nL449:   * Zhang et al. (2025) Y. Zhang, Y. Liang, Z. Zhang, Z. Chen, L. Xiang, Y. Zhao, Y. Zhou, and C. Zong ICDAR 2025 competition on end-to-end document image machine translation towards complex layouts. In International Conference on Document Analysis and Recognition, pp. 505–522. Cited by: cite165†§6.4 .\nL450:   * Zhao et al. (2025) J. Zhao, X. Wei, and L. Bo R1-omni: explainable omni-multimodal emotion recognition with reinforcement learning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.05379. Cited by: cite145†§5.2 .\nL451:   * Zhao et al. (2024) Z. Zhao, H. Kang, B. Wang, and C. He Doclayout-yolo: enhancing document layout analysis through diverse synthetic data and global-to-local adaptive perception. arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.12628. Cited by: cite135†§1 , cite129†§2.2.2 .\nL452:   * Zhou et al. (2017) X. Zhou, C. Yao, H. Wen, Y. Wang, S. Zhou, W. He, and J. Liang EAST: an efficient and accurate scene text detector. In CVPR, pp. 2642–2651. Cited by: cite124†§1 , cite141†§2.1 .\nL453: HunyuanOCR Technical Report\nL454: \nL455: Supplementary Material\nL456: \nL457: This material provides supplementary details to the main paper, including the following sections:\nL458: \nL459:   * •\nL460: \nL461: (cite36†A ) Recommended Instruction\nL462: \nL463:   * •\nL464: \nL465: (cite37†B ) Common Supported IE Categories\nL466: \nL467:   * •\nL468: \nL469: (cite38†C ) Reinforcement Learning Details\nL470: \nL471:     * –\nL472: \nL473: (cite39†C.1 ) Training Configuration\nL474: \nL475:     * –\nL476: \nL477: (cite40†C.2 ) Training Dynamics\nL478: \nL479:     * –\nL480: \nL481: (cite41†C.3 ) Task-wise Performance Improvements\nL482: \nL483:   * •\nL484: \nL485: (cite42†D ) Qualitative examples\nL486: ## Appendix A Recommended Instruction\nL487: \nL488: cite178†Table 7 summarizes the recommended instructions for each task supported by HunyuanOCR, and provides a bilingual (Chinese–English) reference. We recommend using the Chinese instructions to ensure the stability and reproducibility of benchmarking results.\nL489: Table 7: Bilingual (Chinese–English) Instructions Recommended for Different Task Types.\nL490: Task  | Chinese  | English\nL491: Spotting  | 检测并识别图片中的文字，将文本坐标格式化输出。  | Detect and recognize text in the image, and output the text coordinates in a formatted manner.\nL492: Parsing  | 识别图片中的公式，用 L a T e X格式表示。  | Identify the formula in the image and represent it using L a T e X format.\nL493:  | 把图中的表格解析为 HTML。  | Parse the table in the image into HTML.\nL494:  | 解析图中的图表，对于流程图使用 Mermaid 格式表示，其他图表使用 Markdown 格式表示。  | Parse the chart in the image; use Mermaid format for flowcharts and Markdown for other charts.\nL495:  | 提取文档图片中正文的所有信息用 markdown 格式表示，其中页眉、页脚部分忽略，表格用 html 格式表达，文档中公式用 L a T e X格式表示，按照阅读顺序组织进行解析。  | Extract all information from the main body of the document image and represent it in markdown format, ignoring headers and footers. Tables should be expressed in HTML format, formulas in the document should be represented using L a T e X format, and the parsing should be organized according to the reading order.\nL496:  | 提取图中的文字。  | Extract the text in the image.\nL497: Information Extraction  | 输出 $\\mathrm{Key}$ 的值。  | Output the value of $\\mathrm{Key}$.\nL498:  | 提取图片中的: [‘key1’,‘key2’, …] 的字段内容，并按照 JSON 格式返回。  | Extract the content of the fields: [‘key1’,‘key2’, …] from the image and return it in JSON format.\nL499:  | 提取图片中的字幕。  | Extract the subtitles from the image.\nL500: Translation  | 先解析文档，再将文档内容翻译为中文，其中页眉、页脚忽略，公式用 L a T e X格式表示，表格用html格式表示。  | First parse the document, then translate its content into Chinese. Ignore headers and footers; represent equations in L a T e X; and render tables in HTML format.\nL501:  | 提取图中文字，并将其翻译成中文/英文。  | Extract all text from the image and translate it into Chinese/English.\nL502: ## Appendix B Common Supported IE Categories\nL503: \nL504: cite98†Table 8 summarizes the common card and receipt types covered by the 30 IE tasks. The card side includes more than ten categories, such as ID cards, band cards, passports, social security cards, business licenses, driver’s licenses, vehicle licenses, etc.. The receipt side also spans over ten types, including shopping receipts, taxi receipts, VAT invoices, train tickets, bus tickets, itineraries, bank slips, etc..\nL505: Table 8: Common document categories for IE tasks, grouped into Cards & Certificates and Receipts.\nL506: Cards & Certificates  | Receipts\nL507: ID cards  | Shopping receipts\nL508: Bank cards  | Taxi receipts\nL509: Social security cards  | Ferry tickets\nL510: Passports  | Train tickets\nL511: Household registers  | Bus tickets\nL512: Mainland travel permits  | Itineraries\nL513: Business licenses  | Express waybills\nL514: Driver’s licenses  | VAT invoices\nL515: Public institution certificates  | Bank slips\nL516: Vehicle licenses  | Medical inspection reports\nL517: Professional qualification certificates  | Prescriptions\nL518: Tax registration certificates  | Medical records\nL519: Medical insurance vouchers  | Checks\nL520: Road transport certificates  | Ride-hailing itineraries\nL521: Vehicle certificates of conformity  | Takeout orders\nL522: ## Appendix C Reinforcement Learning Details\nL523: \nL524: In this section, we provide additional details on the reinforcement learning (RL) stage of HunyuanOCR beyond the description in the main text cite25†Sec. 5.2 . We first summarize the RL training configuration (cite39†Sec. C.1 ), then present the training dynamics (cite40†Sec. C.2 ), and finally analyze the performance improvements brought by RL training (cite41†Sec. C.3 ).\nL525: ### C.1 Training Configuration\nL526: The detailed training setup for RL is listed in cite179†Tab. 9 . We adopt a constant learning rate schedule with Adam optimizer, a large global batch size, and long-context settings to fully exploit the long-document understanding capability of HunyuanOCR. No explicit KL penalty is applied during RL, allowing the policy to adjust more freely under the guidance of task-specific rewards.\nL527: For rollout generation, we use a low temperature of 0.85 and sample $N=8$ responses per prompt to obtain a diverse set of candidates for reward evaluation and policy updates.\nL528: ### C.2 Training Dynamics\nL529: \nL530: The training dynamics of the RL stage are visualized in cite180†Fig. 4 , where we track two key statistics: the proportion of samples receiving reward 1 at each step, and the mean reward value. As training progresses, the mean reward increases steadily. This consistent upward trend indicates that the policy gradually learns to produce outputs that better satisfy the task-specific reward criteria, validating the effectiveness and stability of the RL process.\nL531: ### C.3 Task-wise Performance Improvements\nL532: \nL533: After RL training, we observe substantial gains across multiple OCR-related tasks.\nL534: Spotting. The spotting ability of HunyuanOCR improves significantly, especially on Art and Screen scenarios, where the scores increase by more than 2 points. We attribute these gains to the rule-based reward design for the spotting task, which can assess the discrepancy between the predicted outputs and ground-truth annotations at a fine-grained level. This encourages the model to simultaneously improve both the accuracy of the predicted bounding boxes and the correctness of the recognized text.\nL535: Parsing. For the parsing task, the score on OmniDocBench increases from 92.5 to 94.1 after RL training. This improvement further demonstrates the effectiveness of the rule-based reward design, which precisely measures content consistency between the model’s outputs and the reference text.\nL536: Information Extraction, VQA, and Translation. In addition, the information extraction (IE) task improves by about 2 points, the average score on OCRBench increased by 3.3, and the text image translation task also shows noticeable gains. These results indicate that the LLM-as-a-judge–based reward design can effectively guide the model to produce more faithful and semantically accurate outputs in higher-level understanding tasks.\nL537: Discussion. In summary, we attribute the effectiveness of RL in HunyuanOCR primarily to two factors:\nL538: \nL539:   * •\nL540: \nL541: High-quality training data. Carefully curated and diverse RL training data provide a solid foundation for the model to learn robust behaviors across spotting, parsing, IE, and translation scenarios.\nL542: \nL543:   * •\nL544: Fine-grained reward design. Task-specific, fine-grained reward functions (both rule-based and LLM-as-a-judge–based) allow the model to receive precise feedback on multiple aspects of its outputs, leading to balanced improvements in recognition accuracy, structural parsing, and semantic understanding.\nL545: \nL546: These elements work together to make the RL stage an effective complement to supervised training, yielding a more capable and reliable HunyuanOCR model.\nL547: \nL548: Table 9: Training setup for reinforcement learning.\nL549: Setting  | Value\nL550: Actor\nL551: Learning rate  | 8e-7\nL552: Micro batch size per GPU  | 1\nL553: Optimizer  | Adam\nL554: Lr schedule  | Constant\nL555: zero-stage  | 3\nL556: Global batch size  | 512\nL557: Max prompt length  | 6144\nL558: Max response length  | 16384\nL559: KL loss coefficient  | 0\nL560: Rollout\nL561: Temperature  | 0.85\nL562: N  | 8\nL563: Top-p  | 0.95\nL564: Tok-k  | 50\nL565: \nL566: Figure 4: Training dynamics of RL. We show the proportions of all-one rewards and the mean reward value, which increases steadily over the course of training.\nL567: ## Appendix D Qualitative examples\nL568: \nL569: Input imagecite181†Image: Refer to caption | Visualization of HunyuanOCR outputcite182†Image: Refer to caption L570: \nL571: Prompt: 检测并识别图片中的文字，将文本坐标格式化输出。\nL572: \nL573: HunyuanOCR: <ref>亲爱的</ref><quad>(22,160),(888,788)</quad>\nL574: \nL575: Figure 5: Robust Text Spotting Results of HunyuanOCR on Artistic Font.\nL576: \nL577: Input imagecite183†Image: Refer to caption | Visualization of HunyuanOCR outputcite184†Image: Refer to caption L578: \nL579: Prompt: 检测并识别图片中的文字，将文本坐标格式化输出。\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNews — Google DeepMind (https://deepmind.google/blog/)\nciteturn2306view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://deepmind.google/blog/\",\"pattern\":\"Nano Banana Pro\"}); Total lines: 309\nL0: ## Explore our next generation AI systems\nL1: \nL2: cite0†Explore models L3: \nL4: Gemini\nL5: \nL6: cite1†Gemini Build intelligent agents cite2†Gemini Omni Create anything from anything cite3†Nano Banana Create and edit detailed images cite4†Gemini Audio Talk, create and control audio L7: \nL8: Specialized models\nL9: \nL10: cite5†Veo Generate cinematic video with audio cite6†Lyria Generate high fidelity music and audio cite7†Genie 3 Generate and explore interactive worlds cite8†Gemini Robotics Perceive, reason, use tools and interact L11: \nL12: Open models\nL13: cite9†Gemma Build responsible AI applications at scale L14: ## Our latest AI breakthroughs and updates from the lab\nL15: \nL16: cite10†Explore research L17: \nL18: Breakthroughs\nL19: \nL20: cite11†SIMA 2 An agent that plays, reasons, and learns with you cite7†Genie 3 Ge